.Dd May 29, 2005
.Dt KCM 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kcm
.Nd process-based credential cache for Kerberos tickets.
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Fl cache-name= Ns Ar cachename
.Oo Fl c Ar file \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl config-file= Ns Ar file
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl g Ar group \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl group= Ns Ar group
.Xc
.Oc
.Op Fl Fl max-request= Ns Ar size
.Op Fl Fl disallow-getting-krbtgt
.Op Fl Fl detach
.Op Fl h | Fl Fl help
.Oo Fl k Ar principal \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl system-principal= Ns Ar principal
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl l Ar time \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl lifetime= Ns Ar time
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl m Ar mode \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl mode= Ns Ar mode
.Xc
.Oc
.Op Fl n | Fl Fl no-name-constraints
.Oo Fl r Ar time \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl renewable-life= Ns Ar time
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl s Ar path \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl socket-path= Ns Ar path
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl S Ar principal \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl server= Ns Ar principal
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl t Ar keytab \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl keytab= Ns Ar keytab
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl u Ar user \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl user= Ns Ar user
.Xc
.Oc
.Op Fl v | Fl Fl version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a process based credential cache.
To use it, set the
.Ev KRB5CCNAME
environment variable to
.Ql KCM: Ns Ar uid
or add the stanza
.Bd -literal

[libdefaults]
        default_cc_name = KCM:%{uid}

.Ed
to the
.Pa /etc/krb5.conf
configuration file and make sure
.Nm kcm
is started in the system startup files.
.Pp
The
.Nm
daemon can hold the credentials for all users in the system.  Access
control is done with Unix-like permissions.  The daemon checks the
access on all operations based on the uid and gid of the user.  The
tickets are renewed as long as is permitted by the KDC's policy.
.Pp
The
.Nm
daemon can also keep a SYSTEM credential that server processes can
use to access services.  One example of usage might be an nss_ldap
module that quickly needs to get credentials and doesn't want to renew
the ticket itself.
.Pp
Supported options:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Fl cache-name= Ns Ar cachename
system cache name
.It Fl c Ar file , Fl Fl config-file= Ns Ar file
location of config file
.It Fl g Ar group , Fl Fl group= Ns Ar group
system cache group
.It Fl Fl max-request= Ns Ar size
max size for a kcm-request
.It Fl Fl disallow-getting-krbtgt
disallow extracting any krbtgt from the
.Nm kcm
daemon.
.It Fl Fl detach
detach from console; see
.Sx SERVICE MANAGER INTEGRATION
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
.It Fl k Ar principal , Fl Fl system-principal= Ns Ar principal
system principal name
.It Fl l Ar time , Fl Fl lifetime= Ns Ar time
lifetime of system tickets
.It Fl m Ar mode , Fl Fl mode= Ns Ar mode
octal mode of system cache
.It Fl n , Fl Fl no-name-constraints
disable credentials cache name constraints
.It Fl r Ar time , Fl Fl renewable-life= Ns Ar time
renewable lifetime of system tickets
.It Fl s Ar path , Fl Fl socket-path= Ns Ar path
path to kcm domain socket (ignored when socket-activated)
.It Fl S Ar principal , Fl Fl server= Ns Ar principal
server to get system ticket for
.It Fl t Ar keytab , Fl Fl keytab= Ns Ar keytab
system keytab name
.It Fl u Ar user , Fl Fl user= Ns Ar user
system cache owner
.It Fl v , Fl Fl version
.El
.Sh SERVICE MANAGER INTEGRATION
.Nm
can be run by
.Xr systemd 1
or a compatible service manager in either of the two ways described
below, and either way can be combined with socket activation.
.Pp
The example unit files
.Pa kcm.service.example
and
.Pa kcm.socket.example
in the
.Pa kcm/
directory of the Heimdal source distribution can be used as a
starting point.
.Pp
.Nm
keeps credentials in memory only, so stopping or restarting it
discards all the credential caches it holds.
Do not arrange for it to be stopped when idle.
.Ss Type=forking
When started with
.Fl Fl detach ,
the process started by the service manager does not exit until the
daemon process that it starts is ready to serve requests, so the
service manager's notion of when
.Nm
is ready is accurate.
The daemon writes its process ID to
.Pa /var/run/kcm.pid ,
or to
.Pa kcm.pid
in the directory named by the
.Ev HEIM_PIDFILE_DIR
environment variable.
Use this with
.Li Type=forking
services, and set
.Li PIDFile=
accordingly (e.g.,
.Li PIDFile=/run/kcm.pid
where
.Pa /var/run
is a symbolic link to
.Pa /run ) .
.Ss Type=notify
When the
.Ev NOTIFY_SOCKET
environment variable is set,
.Nm
sends an
.Xr sd_notify 3
.Li READY=1
message to the service manager once it is ready to serve requests.
Use this with
.Li Type=notify
services, with or without
.Fl Fl detach .
With
.Fl Fl detach ,
the process started by the service manager waits until the daemon
process is ready, then sends
.Li READY=1
and a
.Li MAINPID=
message naming the daemon process, and exits, so the service manager
tracks the daemon process from then on.
.Pp
.Nm
sends no other notifications, and in particular no
.Li WATCHDOG=1
messages, so do not set
.Li WatchdogSec=
for it.
.Ss Socket activation
.Nm
supports systemd-style socket activation (see
.Xr sd_listen_fds 3 ) .
When started with sockets passed in this way it uses them instead of
creating its own, and the
.Fl Fl socket-path
option is ignored.
.Pp
If any of the passed sockets is named
.Dq kcm
(e.g., with
.Li FileDescriptorName=kcm
in the socket unit) then only those sockets are used; otherwise all
of the passed sockets are used.
Only listening Unix domain stream sockets are used (i.e., the socket
unit must use
.Li ListenStream=
with a path, and
.Li Accept=no ,
the default).
Any other passed sockets are closed with a warning, sockets not
selected by name are closed silently, and
.Nm
exits with an error if none of the passed sockets are usable.
.Pp
Clients look for the socket at
.Pa /var/run/.heim_org.h5l.kcm-socket ,
or at
.Pa .heim_org.h5l.kcm-socket
in the directory named by the
.Ev HEIM_IPC_DIR
environment variable, so the socket unit must listen there (e.g.,
.Li ListenStream=/run/.heim_org.h5l.kcm-socket
where
.Pa /var/run
is a symbolic link to
.Pa /run ) .
The socket must be accessible to all users (e.g.,
.Li SocketMode=0666 ) ,
as
.Nm
does its own access control based on the credentials of the connecting
process.
.Pp
SSSD's
.Nm sssd-kcm
uses the same socket path by default, so only one of the two can be
in use at a time.
.Pp
Socket activation works with both
.Li Type=forking
and
.Li Type=notify ;
with
.Fl Fl detach
the passed socket is handed to the detached daemon process.
